The Slow Seed


Leo's grandfather had a saying: "The axe that falls today was sharpened yesterday." He never understood it until the summer he tried to grow a garden on the rocky patch behind their house.
Everyone told him it was a waste of time. The soil was thin, laced with stones, and the neighbors' gardens — planted in richer earth — were already green by the time he'd cleared his first row. He spent whole afternoons pulling rocks the size of fists from the ground, his palms blistering, his back aching by sundown.
His brother Tom would lean against the fence and laugh. "You could've planted in the yard like normal people."
"This is the yard I have," he said, and kept digging.
Weeks passed. He hauled compost from the kitchen scraps, turned the soil until his  arms shook, and planted rows of beans and squash long after the other gardens had already begun to flower. Nothing came up for what felt like forever. He watered anyway. He weeded anyway. Some mornings he stood over the bare dirt and wondered if he was only proving his brother right.
Then, in the second week of July, a single green shoot broke the surface — crooked, stubborn, alive. Within days there were dozens more, pushing up through soil that had once been nothing but stone.
By August, Leo's garden had outgrown every other one on the street. The beans climbed higher, the squash grew fatter, because the roots beneath them had been forced to dig deep for what they needed. What looked like the worst plot of land had become, through sheer persistence, the richest.
